/**
@defgroup api API Reference
@brief Everything a program linking against libmp3lame may use.

The public interface is declared in @c include/lame.h. It is the only header
that LAME installs. Nothing else in the source tree is part of the interface,
and nothing else has a stability or ABI guarantee.

The sections below are in the order a program uses them: create an encoder,
choose the encoding parameters, then pass in audio and collect MP3 frames.
Set up the tags before the first frame is encoded. Read the statistics at the
end, before the encoder is released. The decoder does not depend on any of
this.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_encoding Encoding
@ingroup api
@brief Creating an encoder, running it, and releasing it.

These functions create an encoder, check its settings, encode audio, finish
the stream and release the encoder. The group also has functions that print
the final configuration.

The order of calls is: lame_init(), then any number of the functions in
@ref api_settings, then lame_init_params(). lame_init_params() checks whether
the combination of settings is usable. Audio goes in through one of the
lame_encode_buffer() functions. lame_encode_flush() returns the last frames.
lame_close() releases the encoder instance.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_settings Settings
@ingroup api
@brief The encoder parameters, as set/get pairs.

Every encoder setting that a caller can change is a set/get pair. A setter
stores a value and checks only that value. lame_init_params() later checks
whether the combination of values is usable.

These rules apply to every function in this group:

- a setter returns 0 on success. It returns -1 if it rejects the value or if
  the instance is not usable. A setter that returns other values lists them
  on its own page.
- **a getter cannot report an error.** If the instance is not usable, the
  getter returns 0. For most settings, 0 is also a valid value, so the caller
  cannot tell the two cases apart. Check the instance before you call a
  getter.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_tags Tags
@ingroup api
@brief The ID3 metadata written with the audio.

These functions set what goes into the ID3 tags of the encoded file: the
common fields, any ID3v2 frame, and cover art. They only store what to write.
The encoder writes the tags during encoding, or returns them to the caller on
request.

These rules apply to every function in this group:

- **set all of this first**, before lame_init_params(). lame_init_params()
  builds the ID3v2 tag, and the tag comes before the audio.
- id3tag_init() and the functions after it choose which tags are written. By
  default LAME writes an ID3v1 tag. It also writes an ID3v2 tag as soon as a
  field is set that ID3v1 cannot hold.
- **ID3v1 has fixed-length fields.** Text longer than a field is cut to fit,
  without a warning. ID3v2 has no such limit, so the same call can lose text
  in one tag and keep all of it in the other.
- a setter that returns @c void cannot report a failure, not even a failed
  allocation. A setter that returns @c int returns 0 on success and non-zero
  on failure.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_statistics Statistics
@ingroup api
@brief What the encoder did, counted while it did it.

The encoder counts as it writes frames, and these functions return the counts:
how the bitrates, the stereo modes and the block types were distributed over
the encode. Reading them costs nothing and changes nothing.

These rules apply to every function in this group:

- **when to read them.** The counts are stored in the encoder instance, so the
  order is lame_encode_flush(), then these functions, then lame_close().
  Releasing the instance also releases the counts.
- **lame_init_bitstream() sets them to 0.** lame_init_params() calls it too,
  so the counts describe the current stream, not the whole process.
- **they cannot report an error.** If the instance is not usable, they write
  nothing and the caller's array keeps its old contents. So an array that the
  caller did not initialize looks like a result.
- **the 14 bitrate slots are the bitrates of the selected MPEG version**, in
  the order of the standard's table. The bitrate field in a frame header has
  16 values. 0 means free format and 15 is not valid. The other 14 values are
  the 14 slots. lame_bitrate_kbps() returns the bitrate of each slot. In a
  free-format encode, all frames are counted in slot 0 and the other 13 slots
  are 0.
- **the stereo mode slots are** 0 left/right, 1 left/right with intensity
  stereo, 2 mid/side, 3 mid/side with intensity stereo. They count only when
  the output has two channels. A mono encode leaves all four at 0.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_decoding Decoding
@ingroup api
@brief Reading MP3, through the optional decoder library.

libmp3lame does not decode MP3 itself. libmpg123 decodes it. This is an
external library, and LAME can be built without it. A build without libmpg123
encodes everything as usual and cannot decode anything.

When lame_set_decode_on_the_fly() is set, the encoder creates and releases its
own decoder. It does not use the functions below for this. The functions below
are for an application that reads MP3 itself, for example to re-encode it or
to write it as PCM. LAME's own frontend uses them to read MP3 input.

These rules apply to every function in this group:

- the decoder is **a separate object from the encoder**. hip_decode_init()
  creates it. Every function here takes it as the first argument.
  hip_decode_exit() destroys it. These functions do not take an encoder
  instance.
- **a library built without libmpg123 cannot decode.** In such a library,
  hip_decode_init() and hip_decode_init_gapless() return NULL. So the program
  learns this before it reads any input.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_version Version
@ingroup api
@brief Which LAME a program is talking to.

These functions return the version of LAME that a program is linked against:
a numerical form for comparison, and string forms for display or for writing
into an encoded stream.
*/
